Big-O notation describes how an algorithm's resource requirements grow as input size increases. Constant multipliers are ignored because they do not change the growth rate. Therefore, an algorithm that performs \(2n\) operations is classified as \(O(n)\), since its running time grows linearly with the input size.
public class Main {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int n = 5;
        int count = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            count++;
        }

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            count++;
        }

        System.out.println("Operations: " + count);
    }
}
